    val flowersList= arrayListOf<String>("Pink Primrose","Globe Thistle","Blanket Flower","Trumpet Creeper","Blackberry Lily","Snapdragon","Colt's Foot","King Protea","Spear Thistle","Yellow Iris","Globe-flower","Purple Coneflower","Peruvian Lily","Balloon Flower","Hard-leaved Pocket Orchid","Giant White Arum Lily","Fire Lily","Pincushion Flower","Fritillary","Red Ginger","Grape Hyacinth","Corn Poppy","Prince of Wales Feathers","Stemless Gentian","Artichoke","Canterbury Bells","Sweet William","Carnation","Garden Phlox","Love in the Mist","Mexican Aster","Alpine Sea Holly","Ruby-lipped Cattleya","Cape Flower","Great Masterwort","Siam Tulip","Sweet Pea","Lenten Rose","Barbeton Daisy","Daffodil","Sword Lily","Poinsettia","Bolero Deep Blue","Wallflower","Marigold","Buttercup","Oxeye Daisy","English Marigold","Common Dandelion","Petunia","Wild Pansy","Primula","Sunflower","Pelargonium","Bishop of Llandaff","Gaura","Geranium","Orange Dahlia","Tiger Lily","Pink-Yellow Dahlia","Cautleya Spicata","Japanese Anemone","Black-Eyed Susan","Silverbush","Californian Poppy","Osteospermum","Spring Crocus","Bearded Iris","Windflower","Moon Orchid","Tree Poppy","Gazania","Azalea","Water Lily","Rose","Thorn Apple","Morning Glory","Passion Flower","Lotus","Toad Lily","Bird of Paradise","Anthurium","Frangipani","Clematis","Hibiscus","Columbine","Desert-Rose","Tree Mallow","Magnolia","Cyclamen","Watercress","Monkshood","Canna Lily","Hippeastrum","Bee Balm","Ball Moss","Foxglove","Bougainvillea","Camellia","Mallow","Mexican Petunia","Bromelia")
    val flowerDesc = arrayListOf<String>("Pink Primrose (Primula vulgaris): Pink primrose is a perennial herbaceous flower native to Europe and Asia. It is characterized by its rosette of light green leaves and produces delicate,pale pink flowers with a yellow center. Pink primroses prefer partial shade and moist, well-draining soil, making them popular for woodland gardens or shaded borders.","Globe Thistle (Echinops): Globe thistle is a unique perennial flower with spiky, spherical flower heads. The flowers are typically blue or violet, and they bloom in summer, attracting pollinators like bees and butterflies. Globe thistles prefer full sun and well-drained soil, and their interesting texture and form make them a great addition to perennial borders and cutting gardens.","Blanket Flower (Gaillardia): Blanket flower is a colorful and drought-tolerant perennial native to North and South America. Its striking daisy-like flowers come in shades of red, orange, and yellow, often with contrasting rings or patterns in the center. Blanket flowers are excellent for attracting pollinators and are ideal for hot, sunny locations in the garden."," Trumpet Creeper (Campsis radicans): Trumpet creeper, also known as trumpet vine, is a fast- growing woody vine native to the southeastern United States. It produces large, trumpet- shaped flowers in shades of orange or red during the summer, which are attractive to hummingbirds and bees. It can be grown along fences, trellises, or arbors to create a stunning vertical display."," Blackberry Lily (Belamcanda chinensis): Blackberry lily is a herbaceous perennial that belongs to the iris family. It features attractive sword-like leaves and eye-catching flowers that resemble blackberries, with orange or red spots. After flowering, it produces seed pods that open to reveal shiny black seeds, hence the name blackberry lily."," Snapdragon (Antirrhinum majus): Snapdragon is an old-fashioned, colorful annual flower loved for its tubular, snap-like flowers that come in a wide range of colors. The flowers open when squeezed gently, resembling the face of a dragon, hence the name snapdragon. These plants prefer cool temperatures and bloom from spring to early summer."," Colt's Foot (Tussilago farfara): Colt's foot is a perennial herb that produces bright yellow, daisy-like flowers with scales instead of petals. The flowers appear before the leaves emerge in early spring, and the leaves resemble a colt's hoof, which gives the plant its name. It is often found in moist, disturbed areas."," King Protea (Protea cynaroides): King protea is a large and impressive flowering shrub native to South Africa. Its flower heads are enormous and consist of a central cone surrounded by colorful bracts that resemble petals. The flowers come in various shades, including pink, red, and white. King proteas are popular cut flowers and are also grown in ornamental gardens."," Spear Thistle (Cirsium vulgare): Spear thistle is a biennial or perennial thistle native to Europe and parts of Asia. It has spiny, deeply lobed leaves and produces purple or pinkish flowers in summer. While considered a weed in some areas, it can be an important food source for pollinators like bees and butterflies."," Yellow Iris (Iris pseudacorus): Yellow iris, also known as water iris or yellow flag, is a tall, aquatic or marsh-loving perennial. It features striking yellow flowers with three upright petals called standards and three drooping petals called falls. Yellow iris thrives in wet soil or shallow water and adds a splash of color to water gardens or pond edges."," Globe-flower (Trollius europaeus): Globe-flower is a herbaceous perennial native to Europe and Asia. It bears bright yellow, globe-shaped flowers in late spring to early summer. The blooms stand out against the deeply lobed, dark green leaves. Globe-flowers prefer moist, well-drained soil and partial shade, making them an excellent choice for woodland or shade gardens."," Purple Coneflower (Echinacea purpurea): Purple coneflower is a popular and robust perennial native to North America. It has distinctive purple-pink petals surrounding a spiky, coppery- brown center. The flowers are not only attractive to bees and butterflies but are also used in herbal remedies for their immune-boosting properties."," Peruvian Lily (Alstroemeria): Peruvian lily is a beautiful, long-lasting perennial native to South America. It produces clusters of tubular flowers with vivid, striped or speckled patterns in various colors, including pink, orange, yellow, and white. Peruvian lilies make excellent cut flowers and are well-suited for mixed borders and containers."," Balloon Flower (Platycodon grandiflorus): Balloon flower is a hardy, herbaceous perennial originating from East Asia. It gets its name from the balloon-like buds that swell before opening into star-shaped flowers in shades of blue, pink, or white. The flowers resemble little bells and can add a touch of whimsy to any garden."," Hard-leaved Pocket Orchid (Bulbophyllum fletcherianum): Hard-leaved pocket orchid is an epiphytic orchid species found in Southeast Asia. As its name suggests, it has thick, leathery leaves that form pockets where water collects. The flowers are relatively small, brownish, and have interesting, intricate structures, typical of many orchid species."," Giant White Arum Lily (Zantedeschia aethiopica): Giant white arum lily, also known as the calla lily, is a perennial herb native to southern Africa. It features large, arrow-shaped leaves and striking, trumpet-shaped white flowers with a yellow spadix in the center. It's commonly used as a cut flower in floral arrangements and is popular for weddings and special occasions."," Fire Lily (Lilium bulbiferum): Fire lily, also known as orange lily, is a striking perennial lily ative to Europe. It bears clusters of vibrant orange-red flowers with spotted petals in early summer. Fire lilies prefer well-draining soil and are a captivating addition to garden borders and rockeries."," Pincushion Flower (Scabiosa): Pincushion flower is a charming perennial that produces globe-shaped flower heads resembling pincushions. The flowers come in various colors, including shades of blue, pink, purple, and white. They are excellent for attracting pollinators, especially butterflies, and make lovely cut flowers."," Fritillary (Fritillaria): Fritillaries are a diverse group of bulbous plants with pendulous, bell- shaped flowers. The flowers come in various colors and patterns, often featuring intricate checkered or mottled patterns. Some popular species include the snake's head fritillary (Fritillaria meleagris) and the crown imperial (Fritillaria imperialis)."," Red Ginger (Alpinia purpurata): Red ginger is a tropical perennial plant native to Malaysia and the Pacific Islands. It is cultivated for its striking, red or pink cone-like flower heads that emerge from tall, leafy stems. Red ginger is commonly used in tropical floral arrangements and adds a touch of exotic beauty to gardens and landscapes."," Grape Hyacinth (Muscari): Grape hyacinth is a small, bulbous plant known for its dense clusters of small, bell-shaped flowers that resemble tiny grapes. The flowers come in shades of blue, purple, and white and bloom in spring. Grape hyacinths are ideal for borders, rock gardens, and naturalizing in grassy areas."," Corn Poppy (Papaver rhoeas): Corn poppy, also known as the Flanders poppy, is an annual wildflower that grows in disturbed areas and cornfields. It features vibrant red flowers with black centers and has become a symbol of remembrance for fallen soldiers after World War I."," Prince of Wales Feathers (Amaranthus cruentus): Prince of Wales feathers, or amaranth, is an ornamental annual plant known for its showy, upright spikes of feathery flowers in shades of red, orange, and green. The colorful plumes give the impression of regal feathers, hence the name Prince of Wales feathers."," Stemless Gentian (Gentiana acaulis): A perennial plant with stunning deep blue, trumpet- shaped flowers. The stemless gentian blooms in late summer and early fall, and its flowers are known for their intense color and attractive appearance. It is native to mountainous regions and requires well-drained soil and ample sunlight."," Artichoke (Cynara cardunculus): While the artichoke plant is grown primarily for its edible flower buds, it also produces attractive purple thistle-like flowers. Native to the Mediterranean, the artichoke has large, silvery-green, deeply lobed leaves that add an ornamental aspect to gardens."," Canterbury Bells (Campanula medium): A biennial plant known for its bell-shaped flowers that come in various shades of blue, pink, and white. The Canterbury bells grow in upright spikes and are popular for cottage gardens. They require fertile, well-drained soil and prefer cool temperatures."," Sweet William (Dianthus barbatus): A fragrant and colorful perennial often grown as a biennial. It produces clusters of small, vibrant flowers in various colors like pink, red, and white. Sweet William is beloved for its pleasant fragrance and is a favorite in traditional cottage gardens."," Carnation (Dianthus caryophyllus): A classic and popular flower with a rich history dating back to ancient times. Carnations have ruffled petals and come in a wide array of colors. They are known for their long-lasting blooms and sweet fragrance."," Garden Phlox (Phlox paniculata): A tall, perennial plant with dense clusters of fragrant flowers in shades of pink, purple, white, and red. Garden phlox attracts butterflies and hummingbirds and is a beautiful addition to summer gardens."," Love in the Mist (Nigella damascena): An annual flower with delicate, fern-like foliage and distinctive blue or white flowers surrounded by wispy, thread-like bracts. Love in the Mist produces ornamental seed pods, adding interest to the garden."," Mexican Aster (Cosmos bipinnatus): An annual flower native to Mexico, it has feathery foliage and daisy-like flowers in various colors such as pink, white, and crimson. Mexican asters are easy to grow and attract butterflies and bees."," Alpine Sea Holly (Eryngium alpinum): A perennial plant with spiky, silver-blue flowers and thistle-like appearance. It is drought-tolerant and suitable for rock gardens and dry, sunny areas."," Ruby-Lipped Cattleya (Cattleya labiata): An exotic orchid known for its showy, large, and fragrant flowers. Cattleya orchids come in various colors, including shades of pink, lavender, and white."," Cape Flower (Osteospermum): Also known as African daisies, Cape flowers have daisy- like blooms in vibrant colors like purple, orange, pink, and white. They are low-maintenance and thrive in sunny locations."," Great Masterwort (Astrantia major): A herbaceous perennial with unique, star-like flowers surrounded by papery bracts. Great masterwort prefers partial shade and moist soil and is popular for its unusual appearance."," Siam Tulip (Curcuma alismatifolia): A tropical plant with attractive pink, purple, or white flowers. Siam tulips are grown for their decorative inflorescence and are native to Southeast Asia."," Sweet Pea (Lathyrus odoratus): An annual climbing plant cherished for its fragrant and colorful flowers in shades of pink, purple, blue, and white. Sweet peas are often grown along fences or trellises."," Lenten Rose (Helleborus orientalis): A perennial with elegant, nodding flowers that bloom in late winter and early spring. Lenten roses come in various colors and are excellent for adding early-season interest to gardens."," Barbeton Daisy (Gerbera jamesonii): A perennial plant renowned for its large, daisy-like flowers in vibrant colors like red, orange, pink, and yellow. Barbeton daisies are popular as cut flowers and in garden beds or containers."," Daffodil (Narcissus): A spring-blooming bulbous perennial with trumpet-shaped flowers in various colors, often with a central trumpet or corona surrounded by petal-like tepals. Daffodils are deer-resistant and naturalize well.","Sword Lily (Gladiolus): A tall, spiky perennial known for its dramatic, sword-shaped leaves and vibrant, funnel-shaped flowers arranged along the stem. Gladiolus flowers come in various colors and are popular for floral arrangements."," Poinsettia (Euphorbia pulcherrima): A popular holiday plant with bright red, white, pink, or variegated bracts surrounding small flowers. Poinsettias are native to Mexico and are widely used in Christmas decorations."," Bolero Deep Blue (Echinops): A perennial plant with globe-shaped, deep blue flower heads. Bolero deep blue attracts pollinators and adds a touch of architectural interest to gardens."," Wallflower (Erysimum): A colorful and fragrant perennial plant with clusters of small, four- petaled flowers in shades of yellow, orange, and purple. Wallflowers are often used in borders, rock gardens, and containers."," Marigold (Tagetes): Annual or perennial plants with aromatic foliage and bright, cheerful flowers in shades of orange, yellow, and red. Marigolds are popular for their pest-repelling properties and are used in companion planting."," Buttercup (Ranunculus): A group of perennial, herbaceous plants with bright yellow, glossy flowers. Buttercups are native to meadows and grasslands and are recognizable by their shiny, buttery-yellow petals."," Oxeye Daisy (Leucanthemum vulgare): A perennial plant with white, daisy-like flowers and a yellow central disc. Oxeye daisies are hardy and easily naturalize in open fields and gardens."," English Marigold (Calendula officinalis): An annual or perennial plant with cheerful, orange or yellow flowers. English marigolds are valued for their medicinal properties and are used in herbal remedies and skincare products."," Common Dandelion (Taraxacum officinale): A perennial plant with bright yellow flowers and fluffy seed heads. Despite being considered a weed in lawns, dandelions have edible leaves and are beneficial to pollinators."," Petunia (Petunia hybrida): A popular annual flower with trumpet-shaped blooms in various colors, including pink, purple, red, and white. Petunias are easy to grow and are commonly used in hanging baskets and containers."," Wild Pansy (Viola tricolor): Also known as Johnny Jump Up, wild pansies are charming annual or short-lived perennial flowers with small, tricolored blooms in shades of purple, yellow, and white."," Primula (Primula): A diverse genus of perennials with various types of flowers, including drumstick-shaped, bell-shaped, and flat blooms in colors like pink, purple, and yellow. Primulas are popular as early spring bloomers."," Sunflower (Helianthus annuus): A well-known annual plant with large, yellow flower heads and dark centers. Sunflowers are famous for their ability to turn and face the sun, a phenomenon known as heliotropism."," Pelargonium (Pelargonium spp.): Often referred to as geraniums, though not true geraniums, pelargoniums are tender perennials or annuals with colorful, five-petaled flowers in various hues. They are commonly grown in pots and containers."," Bishop of Llandaff (Dahlia): A specific cultivar of dahlia with dark, bronze-colored foliage and bright red flowers. Dahlias, in general, are popular for their wide range of colors and forms."," Gaura (Gaura lindheimeri): A perennial with delicate, butterfly-like flowers that come in shades of pink and white. Gaura plants have tall, airy stems that sway gracefully in the breeze."," Geranium (Pelargonium spp.): Often confused with pelargoniums, true geraniums are hardy, perennial plants with attractive, lobed foliage and five-petaled flowers in shades of pink, purple, and blue. They are well-suited for borders and ground cover."," Orange Dahlia (Dahlia): A type of dahlia with vibrant orange flowers. Dahlias are known for their diversity of colors and forms, making them popular in gardens and floral arrangements."," Tiger Lily (Lilium lancifolium): A striking perennial lily with large, recurved orange flowers adorned with dark spots, resembling a tiger's coat. Tiger lilies are easy to grow and add a touch of drama to gardens."," Pink-Yellow Dahlia (Dahlia): A dahlia variety with bi-colored flowers, displaying a combination of pink and yellow hues. Dahlia flowers are prized for their intricate patterns and showy appearance."," Cautleya spicata: Also known as the Himalayan Ginger, Cautleya spicata is a rhizomatous  perennial plant. It belongs to the Zingiberaceae family and is native to the Himalayan region.The plant produces striking inflorescences with bright red bracts and pale yellow flowers. The flowers are tubular and have a unique appearance. This species is appreciated for its attractive blooms and is often grown in shaded areas or woodland gardens."," Japanese Anemone (Anemone × hybrida): Japanese Anemone is a beautiful perennial flower native to China and Japan. It belongs to the Ranunculaceae family. The plant features delicate, cup-shaped flowers in various colors, including pink, white, and shades of purple. Japanese Anemones are loved for their late-season blooming, adding a touch of elegance to the autumn garden."," Black-Eyed Susan (Rudbeckia hirta): Black-Eyed Susan is a popular wildflower native to North America. It belongs to the Asteraceae family. The plant displays bright yellow daisy- like flowers with a dark brown or black center, hence the name Black-Eyed Susan. It is a hardy, drought-tolerant plant that attracts butterflies and bees, making it a favorite in many garden landscapes."," Silverbush (Convolvulus cneorum): Silverbush is a small evergreen shrub originating from the Mediterranean region. It is a member of the Convolvulaceae family. The plant showcases striking silver-green leaves and produces attractive white to pinkish funnel-shaped flowers. Silverbush is often grown for its silvery foliage, which provides an eye-catching contrast in gardens."," Californian Poppy (Eschscholzia californica): The Californian Poppy is a delightful annual or perennial flower native to California and adjacent regions. It belongs to the Papaveraceae family. This plant is famous for its bright orange, cup-shaped flowers that bloom in spring and summer. It is easy to grow and serves as an excellent choice for wildflower meadows and naturalistic gardens."," Osteospermum: Osteospermum, commonly known as African Daisy or Cape Daisy, is a genus of flowering plants native to South Africa. It belongs to the Asteraceae family. Osteospermum produces daisy-like flowers in a wide array of colors, including purple, pink, white, and yellow. This perennial plant is appreciated for its long-lasting blooms and tolerance to heat and drought."," Spring Crocus (Crocus vernus): Spring Crocus is a genus of perennial flowering plantsbelonging to the Iridaceae family. These plants are native to Europe and the Middle East.  Spring Crocus displays goblet-shaped flowers in various colors, such as purple, white, yellow,and lavender. They are among the earliest flowers to bloom in the spring, often emerging through the snow."," Bearded Iris (Iris germanica): Bearded Iris, also known as German Iris, is a popular and beautiful perennial flower native to Europe. It belongs to the Iridaceae family. The plant features sword-like leaves and large, showy flowers with three upright petals called standards and three drooping petals called falls. Bearded Iris comes in a wide range of colors and patterns, adding elegance and charm to garden beds."," Windflower (Anemone hupehensis): Windflower, also known as Japanese Anemone, is a lovely perennial plant native to China. It belongs to the Ranunculaceae family. The plant produces delicate, cup-shaped flowers in shades of pink or white. Windflowers bloom in latesummer to fall, making them a valuable addition to extend the flowering season in the garden."," Moon Orchid (Phalaenopsis spp.): Moon Orchid is a genus of stunning orchids native to Southeast Asia and Australia. They are epiphytic or lithophytic plants belonging to the Orchidaceae family. Moon Orchids have broad, waxy petals and sepals in various colors, often featuring intricate patterns and markings. These orchids are popular as indoor houseplants and prized for their exotic beauty."," Tree Poppy (Dendromecon rigida): Also known as the bush poppy, the tree poppy is an evergreen shrub with bright yellow, saucer-shaped flowers that bloom in late winter to spring. It is native to California and thrives in well-draining soils and full sun."," Gazania (Gazania spp.): Gazanias are colorful, low-growing perennials native to South Africa. They are popular for their daisy-like flowers that come in various shades of orange, yellow, pink, and white. They are drought-tolerant and prefer full sun."," Azalea (Rhododendron spp.): Azaleas are a group of flowering shrubs that belong to the Rhododendron family. They are prized for their showy, fragrant flowers that come in variouscolors like pink, purple, red, and white. Azaleas prefer acidic soil and partial shade."," Water Lily (Nymphaea spp.): Water lilies are aquatic plants that float on the water's surface. They have large, round leaves and elegant flowers that come in various colors like white, pink, and yellow. Water lilies are well-adapted to still or slow-moving water and are a favorite in water gardens."," Rose (Rosa spp.): Roses are one of the most popular and beloved flowering plants in the world. They come in an array of colors and varieties, each with its own unique fragrance. Roses are versatile and can be grown in gardens, containers, or as cut flowers."," Thorn Apple (Datura spp.): Also known as jimsonweed, thorn apple is a group of herbaceous plants with trumpet-shaped flowers that can be white, yellow, or purple. They are highly toxic and should be handled with care."," Morning Glory (Ipomoea spp.): Morning glories are climbing vines with trumpet-shaped flowers that come in various colors. They are known for their rapid growth and ability to cover fences, trellises, and other structures."," Passion Flower (Passiflora spp.): Passion flowers are exotic-looking vines with unique, intricate flowers. They come in different colors and have distinctive structures, including a central disc, filaments, and colorful petals. Some species produce edible fruits called passionfruit."," Lotus (Nelumbo spp.): Lotuses are aquatic plants with striking, large, and beautiful flowers that typically rise above the water surface. They have cultural and religious significance in many Asian countries."," Toad Lily (Tricyrtis spp.): Toad lilies are shade-loving perennials known for their orchid- like, spotted flowers that resemble toads skin. They bloom in late summer and early autumn."," Bird of Paradise (Strelitzia reginae): Bird of Paradise is a striking tropical plant with unique, bird-like orange and blue flowers. It is native to South Africa and is often grown as an ornamental plant in warmer regions."," Anthurium (Anthurium spp.): Anthuriums are tropical plants with glossy, heart-shaped leaves and colorful, waxy flowers in various shades of red, pink, and white. They are commonly grown as houseplants."," Frangipani (Plumeria spp.): Frangipani, also known as Plumeria, is a tropical tree known for its fragrant and attractive flowers. The flowers come in various colors, including white, pink, yellow, and red."," Clematis (Clematis spp.): Clematis are climbing plants with showy, star-shaped flowers that come in various colors. They are great for covering fences, walls, and trellises in gardens."," Hibiscus (Hibiscus spp.): Hibiscus is a large genus of flowering plants with vibrant, trumpet- shaped flowers in a wide range of colors. They are popular ornamental plants and are also used to make hibiscus tea."," Columbine (Aquilegia spp.): Columbines are perennial plants with unique, bell-shaped flowers that come in various colors. They are attractive to hummingbirds and butterflies."," Desert-Rose (Adenium spp.): Desert-rose is a succulent plant with striking pink or red flowers. It is native to arid regions and is well-suited to hot and dry conditions."," Tree Mallow (Lavatera spp.): Tree mallows are flowering shrubs with showy, hollyhock- like flowers that come in various colors. They are drought-tolerant and prefer full sun."," Magnolia (Magnolia spp.): Magnolias are large, majestic trees or shrubs with beautiful, fragrant flowers. They come in various shapes and colors and are often associated with elegance and beauty.","Cyclamen (Cyclamen spp.): Cyclamens are small, tuberous plants with attractive, swept-back petals and variegated leaves. They are popular as indoor and outdoor ornamental plants."," Watercress (Nasturtium officinale): Watercress is a semi-aquatic plant with small, round leaves and white flowers. It is edible and often used in salads and other dishes.","Monkshood (Aconitum spp.): Monkshood is a group of flowering plants with hooded flowers that are typically blue or purple. They are highly toxic and should be handled with caution."," Canna Lily (Canna spp.): Canna lilies are tropical plants with large, paddle-shaped leaves and vibrant, iris-like flowers that come in various colors. They thrive in warm climates and prefer moist soil."," Hippeastrum (Hippeastrum spp.): Hippeastrums, also known as amaryllis, are bulbous plants with large, trumpet-shaped flowers that come in various colors. They are commonly grown indoors and bloom during the winter months."," Bee Balm (Monarda spp.): Bee balm is a herbaceous perennial with attractive, tubular flowers that are highly attractive to bees and other pollinators. It is also known as bergamot."," Ball Moss (Tillandsia recurvata): Ball moss is an epiphytic plant that grows on tree branches and other surfaces. It has small, rounded clusters of gray-green leaves and can produce inconspicuous flowers."," Foxglove (Digitalis purpurea): Foxglove is a biennial plant with tall spikes of tubular flowers that come in various colors, such as purple, pink, white, and yellow. It contains toxic compounds and should be handled with care."," Bougainvillea (Bougainvillea spp.): Bougainvillea is a climbing plant known for its vibrant, papery bracts that come in shades of pink, purple, orange, and other colors. It is commonly grown in warm climates as an ornamental plant."," Camellia (Camellia spp.): Camellias are evergreen shrubs or small trees with glossy leaves and showy, rose-like flowers that come in various colors. They are popular for their winter blooms."," Mallow (Malva spp.): Mallows are herbaceous plants with attractive, cup-shaped flowers that come in various colors. They are often grown as ornamental plants or for culinary purposes."," Mexican Petunia (Ruellia simplex): Mexican petunia is a perennial plant with trumpet-shaped flowers that come in shades of purple, pink, or white. It is a low-maintenance plant and can tolerate a wide range of conditions."," Bromelia (Bromeliaceae family): Bromelias are a diverse group of tropical plants with unique rosette-shaped leaves and vibrant, long-lasting flowers. They are often grown as houseplants or in tropical gardens.")
    override fun onCreate(savedInstanceState: Bundle?) {
        super.onCreate(savedInstanceState)
        binding = ActivityShowResultBinding.inflate(layoutInflater)
        setContentView(binding.root)
        resultIndexArray = intent.getIntegerArrayListExtra("resultIndexArray") as ArrayList<Int>
        resultMaxArray = intent.getIntegerArrayListExtra("resultMaxArray") as ArrayList<Int>
        binding.textView.text = intent.getStringExtra("Title")+" Model Prediction Result"
        var flowerTitleList = ArrayList<String>()
        var flowerPercentageList = ArrayList<String>()
        var flowerDescList = ArrayList<String>()
        flowerTitleList.add(flowersList[resultIndexArray[0]])
        flowerPercentageList.add(resultMaxArray[0].toString()+"%")
        flowerDescList.add(flowerDesc[resultIndexArray[0]])
        if(resultMaxArray[1]>10) {
            flowerTitleList.add(flowersList[resultIndexArray[1]])
            flowerPercentageList.add(resultMaxArray[1].toString()+"%")
            flowerDescList.add(flowerDesc[resultIndexArray[1]])
        }
        if(resultMaxArray[2]>10) {
            flowerTitleList.add(flowersList[resultIndexArray[2]])
            flowerPercentageList.add(resultMaxArray[2].toString()+"%")
            flowerDescList.add(flowerDesc[resultIndexArray[2]])
        }

        val recyclerView = findViewById<RecyclerView>(R.id.recyclerView)
        val adapter = ResultListAdapter(flowerTitleList,flowerPercentageList,flowerDescList)
        recyclerView.setHasFixedSize(true)
        recyclerView.layoutManager = LinearLayoutManager(this)
        recyclerView.adapter = adapter
    }
    override fun onCreateOptionsMenu(menu: Menu?): Boolean {
        menuInflater.inflate(R.menu.about, menu)
        return true
    }
    override fun onOptionsItemSelected(item: MenuItem): Boolean {
        when (item.itemId) {
            R.id.action_about -> {
                startActivity(Intent(this,AboutActivity::class.java))
                return true
            }
            else -> return super.onOptionsItemSelected(item)
        }
    }
}